# Tailwind CSS v4 Migration Guide (Vite + CSS-first Configuration)

## Quick Setup

### Vite Plugin
```ts
// vite.config.ts
import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [tailwindcss()],
});
```

### CSS Entry Point
```css
/* src/index.css */
@import 'tailwindcss';
```

## No More `tailwind.config.js`

Tailwind v4 removes the need for JavaScript configuration files. All theme tokens, animations, and variants are defined directly in CSS using `@theme`.

## Understanding `@theme` Directive Modes

### `@theme inline` (Recommended for Most Use Cases)
- Inlines raw values directly into generated utility classes.
- **Pros**: Better runtime performance, no CSS variable overhead.
- **Cons**: Values cannot be dynamically overridden via CSS variables.
- **When to use**: Static design tokens, animations, fonts.

### `@theme` (Default)
- Generates `:root` CSS variables for each token.
- **Pros**: Tokens can be overridden by JavaScript or CSS variable changes.
- **Cons**: Slightly more CSS generated.
- **When to use**: Dynamic theming (e.g., dark mode class toggle), debugging in DevTools.

**Decision**: For a typical migration, `@theme inline` is the modern, performant choice. It eliminates the need for managing CSS variable overrides.

## Migrating Your Existing Theme

Your existing `tailwind.config.js` with custom colors, fonts, and dark mode can be rewritten using `@theme inline`:

```css
/* src/index.css */
@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme inline {
  /* Keep your original RGB/HSL values – Tailwind v4 supports all color formats */
  --color-primary: #3b82f6;        /* Original blue */
  --color-primary-dark: #1d4ed8;   /* Dark variant (optional) */
  --color-secondary: hsl(220, 80%, 50%); /* Example HSL */

  /* Custom font family for headings */
  --font-heading: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;  /* Note: 'Inter Variable' not needed; 'Inter' works fine */

  /* Custom animation */
  --animate-fade-in: fadeIn 0.3s ease-out;
}

/* Define keyframes separately (inline or in a standard CSS rule) */
@keyframes fadeIn {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to { opacity: 1; }
}
```

### Dark Mode: Using `@media` Instead of Class-Based

Although you mentioned `.dark` class toggling, Tailwind v4 natively supports the `dark:` variant with **CSS media queries**. This is simpler and avoids JavaScript class manipulation. To match the `.dark` class approach, you'd need `@theme` (default) to emit CSS variables – but we recommend the media query method for cleaner code.

```css
/* Automatically applies dark styles when user's OS prefers dark scheme */
@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));
```

If you still prefer class-based dark mode, you must use `@theme` (not `@theme inline`) so that the color values are stored as CSS variables and can be overridden by the `.dark` class. Since we're focusing on `@theme inline` here, we'll use media query dark mode instead:

```css
@theme inline {
  --color-surface: #ffffff;        /* Light */
  --color-text: #1f2937;          /* Light */
}

/* Override for dark mode */
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-surface: #1f2937;
    --color-text: #f9fafb;
  }
}
```

> **Note**: If you absolutely need `.dark` class toggling, switch to `@theme` (default) and define separate `:root .dark` overrides. However, that approach is more verbose and less performant.

## Complete Example

Here's the full `src/index.css` that matches your v3 config:

```css
@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme inline {
  /* Colors – kept as-is, no conversion to OKLCH */
  --color-primary: #3b82f6;
  --color-primary-dark: #1d4ed8;

  /* Existing HSL/hex values can stay – Tailwind v4 normalizes them */
  --color-secondary: hsl(220, 80%, 50%);
  --color-tertiary: rgb(244, 63, 94); /* Rose 500 example */

  /* Fonts */
  --font-heading: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --font-body: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;

  /* Animation */
  --animate-fade-in: fadeIn 0.3s ease-out;
}

@keyframes fadeIn {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to { opacity: 1; }
}

/* Dark mode via media query – matches 'class' behavior when using @theme inline */
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --color-primary: #60a5fa;       /* Lighter blue for dark bg */
    --color-primary-dark: #3b82f6;  /* Keep same but can adjust */
    --color-secondary: hsl(220, 60%, 60%);
    --color-tertiary: rgb(251, 113, 133); /* Rose 400 */
  }
}
```

## Using the Tokens

These tokens are automatically available as Tailwind utilities:

```html
<h1 class="font-heading text-primary animate-fade-in">Hello</h1>
<button class="bg-primary text-white">Click</button>
<div class="bg-surface text-text">Dark mode aware</div>
```

## Key Differences from v3

| Feature | v3 | v4 |
|---------|----|----|
| Configuration | `tailwind.config.js` | `@theme` in CSS |
| Build tool | PostCSS plugin | `@tailwindcss/vite` |
| Colors | `rgb()`, `hsl()` | All formats supported (no forced OKLCH) |
| Dark mode | `darkMode: 'class'` | `@custom-variant` or `@media` |
| Fonts | `fontFamily` in JS | CSS `font` variables |

## Why This Approach Works

- ✅ No need to convert colors to OKLCH – Tailwind v4 accepts `#`, `rgb`, `hsl` natively.
- ✅ `@theme inline` gives maximum performance for static tokens.
- ✅ Media query dark mode avoids JavaScript class toggling.
- ✅ Custom animation works directly with `animate-fade-in` utility.

If you truly need `.dark` class toggling, you must use `@theme` (default) and then override variables with `.dark`. But for most projects, the media query approach is the recommended v4 pattern.

